Older Saxon-Based Words (Old English)
These words form the basic, everyday vocabulary of English and tend to be shorter, more direct, and highly frequent in use.

    Core vocabulary: and, the, with, house, food, hand, night, swim, eat, drink, be, have.
    Family terms: mother, father, brother, sister, wife.
    Basic concepts/objects: earth, head, heart, wood, hill, sun, moon, fire, water.
    Animals (while alive): cow, pig, sheep, calf.

Imported Words (French, Latin, and others)
Imported words often have a more formal or technical feel and were added to the language primarily after the Norman Conquest in 1066 (from French) and during various periods of classical learning (from Latin and Greek).

    From French:
        Government/Law: government, parliament, judge, jury, law, crime, palace.
        Cuisine (cooked meat): beef (from French bœuf), pork, mutton, veal, venison.
        Formal synonyms: liberty (vs. Saxon freedom), mansion (vs. Saxon house), reply (vs. Saxon answer), colour (vs. Saxon hue).
    From Latin (often via French or direct borrowing):
        Academic/Scientific terms: biology, structure, molecule, computer, ratio.
        Abstract/Formal terms: fragile (vs. French frail), maternal, paternal, fraternal, vision.
        Early borrowings (pre-Norman): wine, belt, cup, chalk, school.
    From other languages:
        German (via Dutch/German): kindergarten, delicatessen, angst, doppelganger, kaput.
        Hindi/Urdu (via colonial era in India): jungle, shampoo, pajamas, curry, loot, bungalow.
        Scandinavian (Old Norse): sky, give, take, law, by-law.

Newer Words and Coinages (Modern English)
These are recently created words or existing words given new meanings, often in response to technological, social, or cultural changes.

    Technology & Internet: internet, laptop, website, tweet, app, blog.
    Neologisms: modem, chortle (literary coinage), spam (new usage from a brand name).
    New usages: The modern use of gay to mean homosexual (shifted from happy/joyful), or naughty to mean misbehaving (previously poor/needy).

